.TH SR 1 "14 Sep 2026" "sensor-control" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
sr \- sensor read wrapper for WildlifeSystems
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sr
.I command
.RI [ argument ]
.RB [ \-\-sensor
.IR type ]
.RB [ \-\-sensor_id
.IR id ]
.RB [ \-\-with\-errors ]
.RB [ \-\-mock ]
.br
.B sr
.BR \-\-version | \-v
.SH DESCRIPTION
\fBsr\fR is the standard sensor read wrapper for WildlifeSystems nodes. It runs
installed sensor helper scripts and returns JSON-formatted sensor data augmented
with the node serial number and current timestamp. By default, \fBsr\fR runs
multiple sensors in parallel for improved performance.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B sr list
List all available sensor devices installed on the system.
.TP
.BI "sr " device " list"
List the names the device's driver accepts as an argument, one per line, as
the driver itself reports them. Text, not JSON.
.TP
.BI "sr " device " " sensor
Read a specific sensor from the specified device. The argument is passed to
the driver: one of the names from
.BI "sr " device " list" ,
or
.BR internal ,
.B external
or
.BR mock .
A driver that does not accept it, or cannot produce readings, exits 20, which
\fBsr\fR reports on standard error.
.TP
.BI "sr " device " all"
Read all sensors from the specified device. This is the default when no
argument is given.
.TP
.BI "sr " device " setup"
.TQ
.BI "sr " device " enable"
.TQ
.BI "sr " device " version"
.TQ
.BI "sr " device " identify"
Run the driver's own command, as
.BI "sr " device " list"
does. Its output, messages and exit status are passed on unchanged, and the
timeout described under NOTES does not apply, so a setup that takes a while is
not cut off.
.TP
.B sr all
Read all sensors from all installed devices. Sensors are executed in parallel
with automatic concurrency control. A driver that fails is left out and named
on standard error; see NOTES.
.TP
.B sr external
Read all external sensors only (excludes sensors marked as internal).
.TP
.B sr internal
Read all internal monitoring sensors only.
.TP
.B sr check
Verify that every sensor listed in \fI/etc/ws/expected.sensors\fR is reported by
\fBsr all\fR. Exits 0 when the expected sensors file is present and every
\fBsensor_id\fR it lists appears in the output of \fBsr all\fR, and non-zero
otherwise. Intended for health monitoring of a deployed node.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-sensor " type"
Filter output to only include readings where the \fBsensor\fR field matches
the specified type (e.g., "ds18b20", "dht11_temperature", "bme680_humidity").
.TP
.BI \-\-sensor_id " id"
Filter output to only include readings where the \fBsensor_id\fR field matches
the specified identifier.
.TP
.B \-\-with\-errors
Filter output to only include readings where the \fBerror\fR field is not null
or empty. Useful for monitoring and debugging sensor issues.
.TP
.B \-\-mock
Ask each driver for its \fBmock\fR readings, fixed values in the real output
format, instead of reading hardware. Takes precedence over a device
\fIargument\fR, since a driver's mock command takes no filter. On its own it
reads every device, as \fBsr all \-\-mock\fR does. With \fBinternal\fR or
\fBexternal\fR, readings are kept by their \fBinternal\fR field. Every driver,
\fBsensor-onboard\fR included, provides \fBmock\fR. Where \fBpi-data serial\fR
gives no serial, \fBnode_id\fR is \fImock\fR.
.TP
.BR \-\-version ", " \-v
Print the sensor-control version and exit.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B CONCURRENCY
Controls the maximum number of sensors that run in parallel when using the
\fBall\fR, \fBexternal\fR, or \fBinternal\fR commands. Defaults to the number
of CPU cores, or 4 if that cannot be determined. Set to 1 to run sensors
sequentially.
.PP
Example:
.nf
  CONCURRENCY=8 sr all
  CONCURRENCY=1 sr all  # Sequential execution
.fi
.TP
.B EXPECTED_SENSORS_FILE
Path to the expected sensors file read by \fBsr check\fR. Defaults to
\fI/etc/ws/expected.sensors\fR.
.TP
.B LOG_DIR
Directory for the drivers' logs. Defaults to \fI/var/log/sensor-control\fR.
.TP
.B NODE_JSON_FILE
Path to the node metadata file. Defaults to \fI/etc/ws/node.json\fR.
.TP
.B SENS_DIR
Directory searched for \fBsensor-*\fR drivers. Defaults to \fI/usr/bin\fR;
the integration tests point it at a build tree.
.SH OUTPUT FORMAT
\fBsr\fR outputs a JSON array of readings. Each reading is an object with the
fields of the
.BR sc-prototype (1)
template, in this order.
.TP
.B sensor
The name of the sensor, without whitespace (e.g. "dht11_temperature",
"ds18b20", "onboard_cpu").
.TP
.B device
The physical device model (e.g. "dht11", "raspberry_pi"), or null for a
pseudo-sensor such as "storage_used".
.TP
.B measures
The property measured (e.g. "temperature", "humidity").
.TP
.B value
The reading, or null if the reading failed.
.TP
.B unit
The unit of measurement (e.g. "Celsius", "percentage", "hPa", "Ohms").
.TP
.B node_id
The serial number of the node, as reported by \fBpi-data serial\fR. Filled in
by \fBsr\fR.
.TP
.B sensor_id
The identifier of the sensor, or null if it could not be determined.
.TP
.B sensor_name
A human-readable name from the driver's configuration, or null.
.TP
.B location
Where the sensor is: a GeoJSON Feature, with a Point geometry and
\fBproperties.accuracy\fR; the token "{{node}}" or "{{none}}"; or null if no
location was declared.
.TP
.B deployment_id
The deployment identifier from \fI/etc/ws/node.json\fR, or null. Filled in by
\fBsr\fR.
.TP
.B timestamp
The Unix time at which the sensor was read. If the driver did not set it,
\fBsr\fR fills in the time at which the driver wrote its output.
.TP
.B config
Driver-specific details of the sensor, as a JSON object, or null.
.TP
.B internal
Whether the sensor is inside the enclosure.
.TP
.B error
The reason the reading failed, or null. A reading never has both a value and
an error.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
Missing required commands, a system error, or no readings: the driver printed
nothing, or something other than an array of readings, or no driver produced
readings.
.TP
.B 2
Invalid arguments or sensor name.
.TP
.B 20
The device's driver rejected the argument it was given, for example a sensor
name it does not provide, or could not produce readings. The driver's message
is in its log; see FILES.
.TP
.B 21
No driver named \fBsensor-\fR\fIdevice\fR was found, or it is not executable.
.TP
.B 22
(\fBsr check\fR only) The expected sensors file is missing, unreadable, or
lists no sensors.
.TP
.B 23
(\fBsr check\fR only) One or more expected sensors were not reported by
\fBsr all\fR. The missing identifiers are listed on standard error.
.TP
.B 124
The device's driver did not finish within 10 seconds and was stopped.
.TP
.B 137
The device's driver was still running 5 seconds after being stopped, and was
killed.
.PP
Any other status from
.BI "sr " device
is the driver's own, passed on. A driver's own commands, such as
.BI "sr " device " setup" ,
pass on every status.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
List all available sensor devices:
.nf
  sr list
.fi
.TP
Read the onboard CPU temperature sensor:
.nf
  sr onboard onboard_cpu
.fi
.TP
See what the DHT11 driver measures, then read only its humidity:
.nf
  sr dht11 list
  sr dht11 humidity
.fi
.TP
Read all sensors from the onboard device:
.nf
  sr onboard all
.fi
.TP
Read all sensors from all devices:
.nf
  sr all
.fi
.TP
Read all sensors with limited concurrency:
.nf
  CONCURRENCY=2 sr all
.fi
.TP
Read only external sensors:
.nf
  sr external
.fi
.TP
Filter readings by sensor type:
.nf
  sr all --sensor ds18b20
.fi
.TP
Filter readings by sensor ID:
.nf
  sr all --sensor_id my_sensor_123
.fi
.TP
Check that every expected sensor is reporting:
.nf
  sr check && echo "node healthy"
.fi
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/bin/sensor-*
Sensor helper scripts. Each sensor device has its own script.
.TP
.I /var/log/sensor-control/
Each driver's standard error, appended to \fIdevice\fB.log\fR. Where \fBsr\fR
cannot write there, as when it is run by a user other than root, the messages
go to its own standard error instead. \fBLOG_DIR\fR sets another directory.
.TP
.I /etc/ws/expected.sensors
The \fBsensor_id\fR of every sensor expected on this node, one per line. Blank
lines and \fB#\fR comments are ignored, and \fB{{serial}}\fR is replaced with the
node serial number reported by \fBpi-data serial\fR, so the same list can be
deployed to every node. Read by \fBsr check\fR.
.TP
.I /etc/ws/node.json
Node metadata, shipped by \fBws\-node\fR. If present and it carries a
\fBdeployment_id\fR, that value is copied into every reading. Optional: without
it the \fBdeployment_id\fR field is left null. A malformed file is ignored
rather than treated as an error, so it cannot stop sensors being read. The path
can be overridden with \fBNODE_JSON_FILE\fR.
.SH NOTES
Sensor scripts are subject to a 10-second timeout, after which they are sent
SIGTERM; a script still running 5 seconds later is killed. A script that has
not written its output by then contributes nothing, so a driver that retries a
failing sensor must fit its retries inside this limit if the failure is to be
reported at all.
.PP
\fBsr all\fR, \fBsr internal\fR and \fBsr external\fR leave out a driver that
exits with an error, prints nothing, or prints something other than an array
of readings, and name it on standard error, so one broken sensor does not stop
the rest being reported. A driver that prints readings but exits with an error
is named, and its readings are kept. When no driver produces readings,
\fBsr\fR prints nothing and exits 1.
.PP
Each driver's output is captured in a temporary file, removed when \fBsr\fR
exits. An interrupt or SIGTERM ends \fBsr\fR at once, and stops the drivers
still running.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR sc-prototype (1),
.BR sensor-onboard (1),
.BR jq (1)
